  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nWest northwest flow on the front side of building mid level high\npressure will allow some weak impulses to track across the region\nthrough tomorrow. This will result in a fair amount of mid to high\nclouds, especially across northern counties. Still cannot completely\nrule out a few sprinkles coming of the mid deck, but probability of\nmeasurable rain is very remote. \n\nCenter of the mid level high will move from the mid Mississippi\nValley into the Tennessee Valley Tuesday into Wednesday. Temperatures\nacross the region will peak during this time frame with highs in the\nlower to mid 90s and lows in the lower to mid 70s. \n\nThe ridge will start getting suppressed on Thursday and moreso on\nFriday as a trough develops from eastern Canada into New England. As\nthis happens, it may cause a front to sag into the region which at \nthe very least will bring the potential for some convection. However,\nhot conditions may persist into Thursday, especially across southern\nareas, before easing off a bit area wide on Friday.\n\nIt was early to mid July when temperatures this high were experienced\nacross the region. Very few days reached even 90 in August. So, although\nit is not unusual to be hot in early September, most people will not\nbe acclimated to such high temperatures at this point in time. This \nwill bring about some heat stress, especially for those working \noutside or who do not have access to air conditioned spaces.",
